## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic primitives, and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- atomic_flag basics
- memory_order_acquire/release semantics
- backoff to avoid livelock
- cache-line padding to prevent false sharing

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct { 
    alignas(64) atomic_flag flag; 
} spinlock_t;

void spinlock_init(spinlock_t* s) {
    atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

void spinlock_lock(spinlock_t* s) {
    while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
        // Simple backoff to prevent livelock
        for (volatile int i = 0; i < 100; i++);
    }
}

void spinlock_unlock(spinlock_t* s) {
    atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}

bool spinlock_trylock(spinlock_t* s) {
    return !atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire);
}
```

## Stress Test
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

spinlock_t lock;
long long counter = 0;

void* thread_func(void* arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        spinlock_lock(&lock);
        counter++;
        spinlock_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    spinlock_init(&lock);
    pthread_t t1, t2;

    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, thread_func, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, thread_func, NULL);

    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("Final counter: %lld\n", counter);
    return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests proficiency with vectorized string search, boundary handling for streaming data, and realistic benchmarking on multi-socket servers. It assesses understanding of low-level performance optimization, SIMD instruction sets, and the practical challenges of processing large-scale data streams.

## Key Concepts
- SIMD vectorization (AVX-512 with AVX2 fallback for older hardware)
- Cross-chunk boundary handling and tail management for streaming data
- Mask extraction and index reporting for match positions
- Cache behavior analysis and prefetching strategy design
- Multi-socket NUMA awareness and memory bandwidth considerations

## Code Example
```c
#include <immintrin.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define CHUNK_SIZE 64
#define TAIL_SIZE 31

typedef struct {
    size_t* positions;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} MatchResults;

static inline __m512i broadcast_pattern(const unsigned char* pat, size_t pat_len) {
    __m512i v = _mm512_setzero_si512();
    for (size_t i = 0; i < pat_len && i < 64; i++) {
        v = _mm512_set1_epi8(pat[i]);
    }
    return v;
}

void search_simd(const unsigned char* data, size_t n,
                 const unsigned char* pat, size_t pat_len,
                 MatchResults* results) {
    const size_t vec_width = 64;
    unsigned char tail[TAIL_SIZE] = {0};
    size_t tail_len = 0;

    size_t i = 0;
    for (; i + vec_width <= n; i += vec_width) {
        __m512i v = _mm512_loadu_si512((__m512i*)(data + i));
        __m512i p = _mm512_set1_epi8(pat[0]);
        __m512i cmp = _mm512_cmpeq_epi8(v, p);
        uint64_t mask = _mm512_mask_reduce_u64(cmp);

        while (mask) {
            uint64_t bit = __builtin_ctzll(mask);
            size_t pos = i + bit;
            if (pos + pat_len <= n && 
                memcmp(data + pos, pat, pat_len) == 0) {
                if (results->count < results->capacity) {
                    results->positions[results->count++] = pos;
                }
            }
            mask &= mask - 1;
        }
    }

    tail_len = n - i;
    if (tail_len > 0) {
        memcpy(tail, data + i, tail_len);
    }
    for (size_t j = 0; j < pat_len && tail_len + j < CHUNK_SIZE; j++) {
        tail[tail_len + j] = pat[j];
    }

    for (size_t j = 0; j < tail_len; j++) {
        if (j + pat_len <= tail_len + pat_len &&
            memcmp(tail + j, pat, pat_len) == 0) {
            if (results->count < results->capacity) {
                results->positions[results->count++] = i + j;
            }
        }
    }
}

double benchmark_search(const unsigned char* data, size_t n,
                       const unsigned char* pat, size_t pat_len,
                       int use_simd, size_t iterations) {
    struct timespec start, end;
    MatchResults results = {
        .positions = malloc(n * sizeof(size_t)),
        .count = 0,
        .capacity = n
    };

    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &start);
    for (size_t iter = 0; iter < iterations; iter++) {
        results.count = 0;
        if (use_simd) {
            search_simd(data, n, pat, pat_len, &results);
        } else {
            for (size_t i = 0; i + pat_len <= n; i++) {
                if (memcmp(data + i, pat, pat_len) == 0) {
                    if (results.count < results.capacity) {
                        results.positions[results.count++] = i;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &end);

    free(results.positions);
    return (end.tv_sec - start.tv_sec) + 
           (end.tv_nsec - start.tv_nsec) / 1e9;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Baseline**: Scalar `memcmp`/byte-by-byte search across full dataset
2. **Test Cases**:
   - Pattern at stream start (best case for early exit)
   - Pattern at stream end (worst case, full scan)
   - Pattern distributed uniformly (average case)
   - Pattern absent (negative case)
3. **Metrics**: Throughput (GB/s), latency per match, cache miss rate
4. **Variables**: Pattern length (16/32 bytes), server topology effects

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests memory management discipline, low-level data structures, and thread-safety in constrained environments. The scenario mirrors embedded or driver code where malloc is banned due to determinism and memory overhead concerns.

## Key Concepts
- **Fixed-size pool design**: Pre-allocated array eliminates fragmentation and provides O(1) allocation.
- **In-block free-list**: Each block embeds the next free index, using only 4 bytes overhead per block.
- **Lock-free allocation**: CAS (Compare-And-Swap) enables thread-safe operations without locks on multi-core MCUs.
- **Alignment**: Use 8-byte or 16-byte alignment matching the native CAS width to ensure atomicity.
- **Fragmentation**: Fixed-size pools have zero external fragmentation; internal fragmentation is minimal since all blocks are identical.
- **Multi-thread safety on multi-core MCU**: Use atomic operations (CAS) rather than critical sections to avoid priority inversion and improve real-time responsiveness. Disable interrupts for brief critical sections if CAS is unavailable.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block { uint32_t next; uint8_t payload[64]; } Block;
Block blocks[1024];
atomic_uint head_free;

void init_pool() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1023; i++) blocks[i].next = i + 1;
    blocks[1023].next = -1;
    head_free = 0;
}

void* allocate_block() {
    uint32_t old_head = head_free;
    do { 
        if (old_head == -1) return NULL;
    } while (!CAS(&head_free, old_head, blocks[old_head].next));
    return blocks[old_head].payload;
}

void free_block(void* ptr) {
    Block* b = container_of(ptr, Block, payload);
    uint32_t old_head;
    do { old_head = head_free; }
    while (!CAS(&head_free, old_head, b - blocks));
    b->next = old_head;
}
```

## Validation Plan
1. **Functional tests**: Verify allocate returns valid pointers, free reclaims blocks, and exhausted pool returns NULL.
2. **Boundary tests**: Verify behavior at 0, 1, and 1024 allocations.
3. **Thread-safety tests**: Run concurrent allocate/free from multiple threads and check for leaks or corruption.
4. **Memory corruption tests**: Use MPU/MMU to detect buffer overflows into block metadata.

